c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-16566" :question/problem "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is\n$4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-16x+2y+13=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-16x+2y+13 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-16x)+({y}^{2}+2y)+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-16x)+({y}^{2}+2y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-4x]+[{y}^{2}+2y]+13 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+[(y+1)^{2}-{(1)}^{2}]+13 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-4]+[(y+1)^{2}-1]+13 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}-16+(y+1)^{2}-1+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-16+(y+1)^{2}-1+13 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =-13+1+16 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(2,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-16566/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16566"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934/selection/9953c08c94f5d9e8b420b57d247f7768f2570ba597ec8d04682a85580d4142e5" :answer/id #uuid "6ef70125-c783-53b3-a96c-7e7eae106d88" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934/selection/89e81e61edc51bcd8b1ce98252e8b23bf12cc0a8779101b87099cf50a6bb3857" :answer/id #uuid "ac50ad38-0f5e-5a82-97ee-f3b1c3342d57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934/selection/ca19c26ffde1877c79d66bf7da309be5025c0ec8c718d95953d8c5cfa14dcc54" :answer/id #uuid "b26123fa-8439-5850-bd8f-d4c4ca6badd2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934/selection/a1d3a0bfb4d700856d9fb14d1d861ba0bbc78f4b7d79ba9aec61a9bd1e355235" :answer/id #uuid "bf8f60f3-a538-592a-b749-52d09739dad3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934/selection/2241d0d98a22b3d525c08b2a741725081d8d666dd3966b75a025218a344e943e" :answer/id #uuid "b1c2116e-3239-5bb1-953a-a931536e0350" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ and $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f40db8d3-131e-5be8-981d-b12b19078424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72934/selection/a1d3a0bfb4d700856d9fb14d1d861ba0bbc78f4b7d79ba9aec61a9bd1e355235" :answer-field/choices ["q-72934/selection/9953c08c94f5d9e8b420b57d247f7768f2570ba597ec8d04682a85580d4142e5" "q-72934/selection/89e81e61edc51bcd8b1ce98252e8b23bf12cc0a8779101b87099cf50a6bb3857" "q-72934/selection/ca19c26ffde1877c79d66bf7da309be5025c0ec8c718d95953d8c5cfa14dcc54" "q-72934/selection/a1d3a0bfb4d700856d9fb14d1d861ba0bbc78f4b7d79ba9aec61a9bd1e355235" "q-72934/selection/2241d0d98a22b3d525c08b2a741725081d8d666dd3966b75a025218a344e943e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72934" :question/id #uuid "a0bc6070-452c-59d7-b850-234ba4c8bcd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72934" :question/problem "Determine  the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-4x+16y=-16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-4x+16y & =-16 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+(4{y}^{2}+16y) & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4x)+(4{y}^{2}+16y) & =-16 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+4({y}^{2}+4y) & =-16 \\\\ [(x-2)^{2}-4]+4[(y+2)^{2}-4] & =-16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+4(y+2)^{2}-16 & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4+4(y+2)^{2}-16 & =-16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =-16+16+4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2>1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72934/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72934"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939/selection/a45b3d817136e66dedc99befbbdb23cc00480a3166e8ebd3be46752fa9cd57d5" :answer/id #uuid "8d51df1b-0356-5b96-8a9c-729783b8296d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939/selection/7898b015caa2a5b24b4c9e43acbaaa371c0f47a12e1365574e1322d58326243b" :answer/id #uuid "f6d713da-b08e-51e5-be33-be87cc109799" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939/selection/06ae074fdaa731ba59c595aa34f47654f1abbfff7fd7b3d603ff053ae90714e0" :answer/id #uuid "c177427f-8e9f-539c-9f12-23609c5e21dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939/selection/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" :answer/id #uuid "a20afe78-9e3c-52d7-8996-16a4466b37a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939/selection/cc3b771d69c249b43b6611d0bc746155921a411fbf43e84f275766a62a6727d8" :answer/id #uuid "b55d384a-a2ec-50c2-b99e-0b871e41affb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a013b6-101b-53f6-9554-aa28d954adc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72939/selection/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" :answer-field/choices ["q-72939/selection/a45b3d817136e66dedc99befbbdb23cc00480a3166e8ebd3be46752fa9cd57d5" "q-72939/selection/7898b015caa2a5b24b4c9e43acbaaa371c0f47a12e1365574e1322d58326243b" "q-72939/selection/06ae074fdaa731ba59c595aa34f47654f1abbfff7fd7b3d603ff053ae90714e0" "q-72939/selection/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" "q-72939/selection/cc3b771d69c249b43b6611d0bc746155921a411fbf43e84f275766a62a6727d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72939" :question/id #uuid "b60a8b91-cc5c-53f4-8707-6575410a9274" :question/math-academy-id "q-72939" :question/problem "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+20x-12y=-28.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+20x-12y & =-28 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}+20x)+(2{y}^{2}-12y) & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}+20x)+(2{y}^{2}-12y) & =-28 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+4x)+2({y}^{2}-6y) & =-28 \\\\ 5[(x+2)^{2}-4]+2[(y-3)^{2}-9] & =-28 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}-20+2(y-3)^{2}-18 & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}-20+2(y-3)^{2}-18 & =-28 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+2(y-3)^{2} & =-28+20+18 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+2(y-3)^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-72939/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72939"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-279724/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d2560cc5-0289-57a2-a079-1e715e6688e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279724/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "4ad5d651-651c-5992-8b3e-7c23a42e294e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279724/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e544ccb-c58d-5660-8a1e-14b988b90682" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279724/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-279724/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-279724/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279724/field-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "aa3abd93-9a6a-5457-a2bf-a966b618c6f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279724/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "80ef5694-8eb1-51a4-bee3-f68bc92e984d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279724/field-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-279724/field-2/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279724" :question/id #uuid "7b2ac131-3789-57d6-ae7d-fc4ddec732af" :question/math-academy-id "q-279724" :question/problem "The center of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-36x+50y-1=0$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-36x+50y-1 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-36x)+(5{y}^{2}+50y)-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{x}^{2}-36x)+(5{y}^{2}+50y)-1 & =0 \\\\ 6[{x}^{2}-6x]+5[{y}^{2}+10y]-1 & =0 \\\\ 6[(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}]+5[(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]-1 & =0 \\\\ 6[(x-3)^{2}-9]+5[(y+5)^{2}-25]-1 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-3)^{2}-54+5(y+5)^{2}-125-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x-3)^{2}-54+5(y+5)^{2}-125-1 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-3)^{2}+5(y+5)^{2} & =1+54+125 \\\\ 6(x-3)^{2}+5(y+5)^{2} & =180 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{30}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{30})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-279724/field-1" "q-279724/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279724"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-279726/field-1/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "77a551c8-5f48-51ca-b623-d6de24981b40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-279726/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7732014f-4bb9-57d6-a280-9ba7b1f4d999" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-279726/field-1/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-279726/field-1/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-279726" :question/id #uuid "7fbf523c-93a9-5c29-bfba-96b46bc02f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-279726" :question/problem "For the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}+72x-100y+19=0$, the length of the major axis is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}+72x-100y+19 & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+72x)+(25{y}^{2}-100y)+19 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+72x)+(25{y}^{2}-100y)+19 & =0 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}+8x]+25[{y}^{2}-4y]+19 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+25[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+19 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+4)^{2}-16]+25[(y-2)^{2}-4]+19 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+4)^{2}-144+25(y-2)^{2}-100+19 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x+4)^{2}-144+25(y-2)^{2}-100+19 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+4)^{2}+25(y-2)^{2} & =-19+100+144 \\\\ 9(x+4)^{2}+25(y-2)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5=10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-279726/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-279726"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
